<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis XVIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1755–1824</span> · Also known as Louis XVIII of France</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis XVIII, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1814 to 1824, apart from the Hundred Days of 1815. Restored by the allies after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s abdication, he granted the Charter of 1814, which kept a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with a parliament and preserved many revolutionary and Napoleonic reforms. He fled during Napoleon's return and came back after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>. His brother Charles X succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xviii.jpg" alt="Louis XVIII" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Gérard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A9rard_-_Louis_XVIII_of_France_in_Coronation_Robes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
